From 401c5e57ade947fe4f9294b17592d08c804c95d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.local>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 03:34:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] The judge ruled on 228,469 cells and the registry learned
 1,278 of them

MEASURED ON THE TRADING BOX 2026-09-25. `judged_at` was set on 1,278 candidates in the
desk's entire history, every one of them stamped at the same instant, while the gauntlet
had written 228,469 verdicts. The `gate_verdicts` cursor sat at byte 226,198 of a
54,119,184-byte ledger: 0.42% read, 227,497 verdicts unpoured, and the input growing 113x
faster than the cursor advanced. That is not a lag, it is a divergence -- it never
recovers and every hour makes it worse.

THE CAUSE IS ONE MISSING INDEX. `mark_candidate` is the only writer of `judged_at` and it
matches `WHERE id=? OR donated_cell=?`. `id` has the PK autoindex; `donated_cell` had
nothing, and an OR across one indexed and one unindexed column can use NEITHER, so
EXPLAIN read `SCAN research_candidates` -- 740,357 rows per verdict. Measured on the box:
2.09 rows/s against 87,931 rows/s with the index, a 50,772x fall, and the 227,497-row
backlog goes from 36.5 hours to 2.6 seconds. The index builds in 0.9s.

This is the THIRD instance of one disease. `ix_candidates_gridcell` and `ix_disc_hash`
cured the two WRITE doors; this is the same scan on the READ-BACK door, and it cost the
desk every verdict it ever earned. The forward path was healthy throughout -- 372,563
registry rows reached the docket and the sandbox families were in front of the judge --
so the cells were generated, donated, compiled, judged, and the verdict then evaporated.

WHAT IT WAS WEARING. `registry_sync` ran every hour, exited zero, advanced its cursor and
rewrote its report every pass. Its SELF channel was perfect and its PRODUCT channel moved
too, because the pass really did pour rows: 203 of them, against ~3,500 arriving an hour.
rc=0 with a receipt is this desk's signature failure and neither existing channel in
`check_organ_cadence` could ever have seen it.

SO THE FIX SHIPS WITH THE BREACH CLASS THAT CATCHES IT. `stream_backlogs` measures every
cursored stream as an unread FRACTION of its input -- the honest question for a cursor is
not whether it moved but whether it is keeping up -- and raises STREAM_DIVERGING at 25%
unread on a host that runs the clocks, NO_CURSOR where a receipt is missing entirely. The
real break measured 99.58% unread, four times past the line. A mirror host is not
convicted, a new stream under 2 MB is not convicted, and UNMEASURED stays a verdict.

`VERDICT_ROWS_MULTIPLE` lifts the verdict stream's row cap, which was sized when every
stream cost the same per row. With the index the verdicts are the cheapest stream on the
pass and the wall-clock deadline is still the real guard.

Nothing here caps, shrinks or vetoes anything: it moves earned verdicts back to the rows
that earned them, and every threshold added can only ever demand that MORE cells be
judged.

